---

<Guide
  title="How do you get more Google reviews? | PISMA"
  description="Ask for the review at the right moment, make it easy with a link or QR, and reply to every one — that’s how you rise on Google, Maps and AI tools like ChatGPT."
  slug="more-google-reviews"
  lang="en"
  question="How do you get more Google reviews?"
  sub="A practical guide for every business — no tricks and nothing technical."
  answer="Ask every happy customer for a review the moment they thank you, and make their life easy: a link or a QR that opens Google’s review form directly. Reply to all reviews — the negative ones too — and make sure they come in steadily, a few every month, rather than many bunched up at once. And never buy or stage reviews: Google forbids it, and it can cost you your profile."
  updated="2026-07-28"
  faq={faq}
>
  <h2>Why do reviews matter so much?</h2>
  <p>
    Reviews are among the first things Google looks at when deciding which businesses
    to show high up on Maps and in search. And it’s no longer just Google reading them:
    AI tools like ChatGPT read them too when someone asks “which repair shop can I trust?”
    or “where can I get good pizza near me?”.
  </p>
  <p>They look at three things, and you influence all three:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Volume</strong> — how many customers have shared their opinion.</li>
    <li><strong>Freshness</strong> — when the latest review was written. Recent reviews say “this place is alive”.</li>
    <li><strong>Content</strong> — what customers write. When someone writes “fast service” or “clean space”, Google and the AI tools understand what you offer — in a customer’s words, not yours.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That’s why reviews are also one of the strongest steps if you want
    <a href="/en/guides/get-recommended-by-chatgpt/">ChatGPT to recommend you</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What counts more — the total or the flow?</h2>
  <p>
    The flow. A business with fewer but recent reviews looks alive, while another with
    many that are all old looks like something has changed — and the customer hesitates.
  </p>
  <p>
    Google and the AI tools “think” the same way: a steady flow of new reviews is a sign
    that the place is running and customers are happy today, not last year.
    So don’t treat it as a one-time campaign — make it part of the daily
    routine, like the register.
  </p>

  <h2>How do you ask for a review the right way?</h2>
  <p>
    At the right moment and the easy way. Most customers don’t refuse to write a couple
    of lines — they just don’t think of it themselves, or can’t be bothered to hunt for where.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The right moment:</strong> when the customer thanks you — the minute they pick up the car, see the haircut, finish the meal. That’s when “would you write us a couple of lines?” comes naturally.</li>
    <li><strong>An easy link:</strong> your Google profile gives you a link that opens the review form directly. If you haven’t set up the profile, start with the guide on the <a href="/en/guides/google-maps-profile/">Google Maps profile</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>A QR on site:</strong> a QR at the register, on the table or on the receipt makes the link scannable in seconds, no typing. That’s exactly what <a href="/en/reviews/">The Review Wheel</a> — PISMA’s tool — is built on.</li>
    <li><strong>A personal word:</strong> “a review would really help us” coming from you works better than an impersonal message.</li>
    <li><strong>Same day:</strong> if you send the link by message, send it while the experience is fresh — not a week later.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Should you reply to every review?</h2>
  <p>
    Yes, all of them. A reply shows — to future customers, to Google and to the
    AI tools — that there’s a person behind the profile who listens.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the positive ones, a short thank-you in your own words is enough — not the same
    boilerplate message everywhere. For the negative ones, keep this order:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Don’t reply angry. Let a few hours pass, but reply within 1–2 days.</li>
    <li>Thank them for their time and own up to whatever is actually true.</li>
    <li>Say what you’ll do differently — one sentence is enough.</li>
    <li>Take the conversation out of public view: “give us a call and we’ll make it right”.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A calm reply to a negative review often tells the next customers more
    than a stack of five-star ratings put together.
  </p>

  <h2>What do you never do with reviews?</h2>
  <p>
    Anything that isn’t real. Google’s rules are clear, and breaking them
    can cost you reviews wiped out in bulk or a restricted profile.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Don’t buy reviews</strong> — from “services” that sell them by the batch.</li>
    <li><strong>Don’t have relatives and employees</strong> write as fake customers.</li>
    <li><strong>Don’t give a gift or discount in exchange for a review</strong> — any reward is forbidden.</li>
    <li><strong>Don’t “gate”:</strong> that is, sending only the people you know will write nice things to Google, and the unhappy ones to a hidden form. That’s forbidden too.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Beyond the rules, fake reviews show — to the algorithms
    and to people alike. A few real ones are worth more than many staged ones.
  </p>

  <h2>How does PISMA help?</h2>
  <p>
    The hard part about reviews isn’t the theory — it’s asking consistently, every day,
    without forgetting. That’s why we built <a href="/en/reviews/">The Review Wheel</a>:
    a QR sticker on the table or at the register. The customer scans, spins a
    wheel and wins a gift — and at the moment they’re smiling, we ask them for a couple
    of lines on Google. One tap and the review form is open.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the rules you saw above? They’re respected: the customer wins the gift by
    scanning, whether they write a review or not — it’s not a reward in exchange. We ask
    for the review afterwards, politely and with no strings attached.
  </p>
  <p>
    See how it works through the customer’s eyes in the <a href="/demo/pitsaria/">live demo</a>.
    It costs {eur(autoReviews.price)} per month, with no commitment whatsoever — plus the
    <a href="/en/qr-stickers/">QR stickers</a> one-off. Or start with the
    <a href="/en/free-assessment/">free {ELEGXOS.minutes}-minute Check</a> to first see
    where you stand on Google and AI.
  </p>
</Guide>
